<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Choe Mu-seon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1325">1325</a>–<a href="/years/1395">1395</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Choe Mu-seon was a Korean inventor and military commander of the late Goryeo period. Having learned the making of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> from a Chinese merchant, he persuaded the court to found an office for firearms in 1377 and developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and other gunpowder weapons. In 1380 ships armed with his cannon destroyed a large Japanese pirate fleet at Jinpo.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ch'oe Musŏn was born into a wealthy family in Yeongcheon, in Gyeongsang Province, where his father was an official, and qualified as a military officer through the civil service examination. As a child at the royal palace with his father he saw fireworks made by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and Chinese. He grew up as the Goryeo government lost control of the country and wokou <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> crossing the Korean Strait plundered the coasts and, in the south, raided deep inland, which the generals Yi Sŏnggye and Ch'oe Yŏng could not prevent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ch'oe travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, where the making of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> was kept secret, and learned that it needed sulfur, fine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and potassium nitrate, but not how to obtain niter or prepare the powder. After repeated failures he bribed Li Yuan, a Chinese merchant visiting Goryeo on business, for the recipe, against Mongol and Chinese law, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> began making its own gunpowder between 1374 and 1376. After he demonstrated its power before King U, the government gave him an official laboratory and factory.</p><p>There he developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hand-cannon">hand cannons</a> and other firearms, among them the singijeon and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hwacha">hwacha</a>, a launcher that has been likened to the first modern multiple rocket launcher. He was one of the commanders at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jinpo">Battle of Jinpo</a>, which the Korean forces won with the help of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons, and he began building warships to drive off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>. Two works on gunpowder and guns, the Hwayaksuryeonbeop and the Hwapobeop, are attributed to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His inventions brought him promotion and a part in politics, but he was already old, and soon after the founding of the Joseon dynasty he retired from military and political affairs. He died in 1395, and King Taejo gave him the honorary title of vice-premier. His son Ch'oe Haesan was also a military officer. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> he is remembered as a patriot and a model for scientists.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ch'oe Musŏn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later Goryeo supplied ships and troops for the failed Yuan invasions of Japan in 1274 and 1281, and late in the dynasty <b>Choe Mu-seon</b>'s gunpowder office armed ships with cannon that destroyed a Japanese pirate fleet at Jinpo in 1380.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yeongcheon, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in Haeju, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.97,128.94&amp;year=1370" aria-label="Yeongcheon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Choe Mu-seon"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5616 4466h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5616 4466h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.97,128.94&amp;year=1370">What was here in 1370? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
